\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The left Killing-Clifford module is primary of type #{V_\rho}}

\p{Let #{\mathfrak g} be a finite-dimensional complex semisimple Lie
algebra of rank #{\ell}. Choose positive roots #{\Phi^+}, put
#{r=\lvert\Phi^+\rvert}, and write
#{\rho=\frac12\sum_{\alpha>0}\alpha}. Then

##{\dim\mathfrak g=\ell+2r.}

Give #{\Cl(Q_\kappa)} the left action #{L} of
\ref{fcap-003E}. As a #{\mathfrak g}-module it is

##{\Cl(Q_\kappa)\simeq
2^{\ell+r}V_\rho.}

Here the exponent is the multiplicity; #{\dim V_\rho=2^r}, so the total
dimension is #{2^{\ell+2r}}, as required. For the zero Lie algebra this
reads as the one-dimensional trivial module.}

\p{First compute the spinor constituent. The weights of the adjoint
orthogonal module are zero with multiplicity #{\ell} and the roots in
opposite pairs. The exterior spinor character is therefore

##{\operatorname{ch}S=
2^{\lfloor\ell/2\rfloor}
\prod_{\alpha>0}
\left(e^{\alpha/2}+e^{-\alpha/2}\right).}

The Weyl character formula for highest weight #{\rho}, together with
the Weyl denominator identity applied to #{2\rho} and divided by the
ordinary denominator, gives

##{\operatorname{ch}V_\rho
=e^\rho\prod_{\alpha>0}(1+e^{-\alpha})
=\prod_{\alpha>0}
\left(e^{\alpha/2}+e^{-\alpha/2}\right).}

Complete reducibility and independence of irreducible characters now
give

##{S\simeq2^{\lfloor\ell/2\rfloor}V_\rho.}

\citet{Example 2.5(1), internal preprint p. 8}{panyushev2001exterior}
records this adjoint specialization under the complex semisimple
hypotheses. The character calculation above supplies the comparison in
the fixed normalization rather than substituting that conclusion without
proof.}

\p{It remains to pass from a spinor carrier to the full Clifford
algebra. If #{n=\ell+2r} is even, the matrix range of \ref{fcap-002V}
gives

##{\Cl(Q_\kappa)\simeq\operatorname{End}(S)\simeq S\otimes S^*.}

Left multiplication acts on the first factor, so #{S^*} is a trivial
multiplicity space of dimension #{2^{n/2}}. If #{n} is odd, the odd
structure of \ref{fcap-002W} gives two matrix blocks
#{\operatorname{End}(S_1)\oplus\operatorname{End}(S_2)}. The two full
Clifford actions differ, but their restrictions to the even algebra are
equivalent; since #{\delta(\mathfrak g)} is even, both blocks contain the
same #{\mathfrak g}-module. Their combined multiplicity-space dimension
is #{2\cdot2^{(n-1)/2}=2^{\lceil n/2\rceil}}.}

\p{In both parity cases the multiplicity of #{V_\rho} is

##{2^{\lceil n/2\rceil}
2^{\lfloor\ell/2\rfloor}
=2^{\ell+r}.}

This proves the displayed decomposition.
\citet{Theorem 39, pp. 312--313}{kostant1997clifford} independently
proves the exact left-regular primary-type statement by a highest-weight
right ideal. TauCeti formalizes the selected left action and its commuting right
multiplications, but the character, matrix-block, and multiplicity
synthesis here is not a packaged formal theorem.}

\p{The conclusion is a module decomposition only. It does not assert
the stronger algebra factorization
#{\operatorname{End}(V_\rho)\otimes\Cl(P)}, identify the full commutant,
or invoke the Hopf--Koszul--Samelson primitive algebra. The normal-ordered
#{\mathfrak{gl}_N} calculation in the separate CAR section uses a trace
shift and is not a substitute for this Killing-adjoint theorem.}
